      "full_text": "The Financial Times has reported, in a headline available through a public news listing, that a $14bn data centre associated with Meta and BlackRock exposes lenders to an insurance gap. The supplied evidence contains the headline, not the article body, so the project's location and status, the lenders involved, the coverage structure, the cause of the gap and any possible financial consequence cannot be verified here.\n\nOn the evidence available, the defensible reading is narrow: the Financial Times headline places an insurance gap and lender exposure around a data centre associated with Meta and BlackRock. It does not establish that a loss has occurred or show how large any uncovered exposure might be.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nIf the reported insurance gap affects risks relevant to the financing, it could influence how lenders assess who may ultimately bear those risks. That is a question to test, not a conclusion about loss: the headline does not identify what is outside coverage or which contractual protections may apply.\n\nAny assessment may turn on details that remain unknown, including the nature of the coverage gap and the terms governing lender exposure. Without those details, the potential financial consequence cannot be established.\n\n## What to watch\n\nFurther reporting may clarify what the gap covers, which lenders could be exposed and whether any party attributes a financial consequence to it.\n\nUntil then, the Financial Times headline supports only this narrow proposition: a $14bn data centre associated with Meta and BlackRock exposes lenders to an insurance gap. The size, mechanism and significance of that exposure are unknown from the available headline-only evidence.",
